I’m using the repository’s `crimes-codebase-risk` workflow because this is a frontend refactor investigation driven by `crimes context`. I’ll inspect the actual component and its local token/style setup, then propose—not implement—a finding-by-finding refactor.

The global `crimes` binary isn’t installed in this shell. I’m switching to the repository’s locally built CLI—the documented equivalent—so the context report still comes from the product itself.

`crimes context` confirms medium overall risk: one accessibility finding and two low-risk styling findings. I’d refactor [Card.tsx](/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/07-stress-frontend/src/Card.tsx) along these lines:

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  actionLabel: string;
  onActivate: () => void;
};

export function Card({ children, actionLabel, onActivate }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <article className="w-full max-w-4xl bg-surface p-6 text-ink shadow-sm">
      <div className="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 @md:grid-cols-3">
        <div className="min-w-0 text-lg @md:col-span-2">{children}</div>
        <aside className="text-sm text-ink/70">sidebar</aside>
      </div>

      <button
        type="button"
        onClick={onActivate}
        className="mt-6 text-brand focus-visible:outline-brand"
      >
        {actionLabel}
      </button>
    </article>
  );
}
```

The `@md:` notation assumes Tailwind container-query support; the card would also receive `@container`. A CSS-module container query is the fallback.

- `accessible_interaction_risk`: remove `onClick` from the non-semantic `<div>` and expose an explicit `<button>`. This provides keyboard focus, Enter/Space activation, and an accessible name without manually recreating button behavior. Keeping arbitrary `children` outside the button also avoids invalid nested controls.

- `design_token_escape`: replace inline hex, RGB, shadow, spacing, and typography literals with named utilities backed by [tailwind.config.ts](/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/evals/fixtures/07-stress-frontend/tailwind.config.ts). Existing `brand`, `surface`, and `ink` tokens cover the main palette; any intentionally retained border or shadow should first become a shared semantic token rather than another component-local literal.

- `responsive_fragility`: replace `width: 800`, `width: 720`, fixed height, and the 240px sidebar with `w-full`, a capped `max-w-*`, automatic height, and a container-responsive grid that collapses to one column. Tokenized `text-lg`/`text-sm` replaces fixed pixel typography.

Because `crimes context` found no likely tests, I’d add a component test covering keyboard activation and accessible naming, plus a narrow-container layout test or visual story before implementing the refactor.
